==========================================================================
SECTION 1 -- THE TOOLCHAIN, AND WHAT IT CANNOT DO.  Printed before
the first number because a claim about a decoder needs one.
==========================================================================

  compiler:      Ubuntu clang version 21.1.8 (6ubuntu1)
  disassembler:  Ubuntu LLVM version 21.1.8

  LLVM IR is the ORACLE in this course and never a dependency.  Every
  number below is produced by code in this directory: the IR reader is
  ours, the selector is ours, the allocators are ours, the scheduler
  is ours, the encoder is ours, the timing harness is ours.  Section 2
  prints what replaces each piece of LLVM, by FILE NAME.

THE FOUR THINGS THIS HOST CANNOT DO, and each one changes what a
claim in this course is allowed to be:

  * x86-64 RUNS NATIVELY.  There is an x86-64 machine, so this course
    can put a RATIO on the cost of a spill and a RATIO on the cost of
    a schedule.  NO OTHER COURSE IN THIS COLLECTION HAS DONE THAT, and
    it is the one measurement that belongs here and nowhere else.
  * aarch64-linux-gnu COMPILES AND CANNOT RUN.
    aarch64-linux-gnu-ld IS NOT INSTALLED: no linker, so nothing this
    course emits for this target is EVER EXECUTED and no
    relocation it emits is EVER RESOLVED.
    NO EMULATOR: qemu-aarch64 and spike are both ABSENT.

  * riscv64-linux-gnu COMPILES AND CANNOT RUN.
    riscv64-linux-gnu-ld IS NOT INSTALLED: no linker, so nothing this
    course emits for this target is EVER EXECUTED and no
    relocation it emits is EVER RESOLVED.
    NO EMULATOR: qemu-riscv64 and spike are both ABSENT.

CONSEQUENCE, and it governs every label in the file:
  * every claim about x86-64 CODE is MEASURED, because it ran;
  * every claim about an AArch64 or RISC-V INSTRUCTION is
    MEASURED-ON-BYTES, because a word was emitted and read back and
    nothing more;
  * every claim about what a machine WOULD DO with those words is
    QUOTED, with a document named.


==========================================================================
SECTION 2 -- WHAT AN IR BUYS.  The ratio, and the half nobody states.
==========================================================================

THE POSITIVE HALF.  One source, five optimisation levels, x86-64.  For
each level: how many IR instructions clang emitted, how many machine
instructions the second reader printed, and the RATIO.

  THE RATIO IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT FOR AN IR, and it is read in both
  directions.  ABOVE 1.0 the IR is bigger than the code: the backend
  deleted instructions the IR had.  BELOW 1.0 the code is bigger: the
  backend inserted ones -- an address computation, a zero extension,
  a spill reload.  Both directions are decisions, and the report says
  which side each level is on rather than quoting one average.

  level   IR insns   machine insns   IR/machine
  O0           260             235        1.106
  O1            99             117        0.846
  O2           207             253        0.818
  O3           207             253        0.818
  Os            99             103        0.961

  the ratio spans 0.818 to 1.106 across five levels, and it is NOT
  MONOTONIC: -O1 gives a SMALLER ratio than -O0 because -O0 emits
  MORE machine instructions than IR and -O1 emits fewer. §KEEP§A
  NUMBER THAT MOVES IN BOTH DIRECTIONS IS A MEASUREMENT; A NUMBER
  THAT ONLY FALLS AS THE COMPILER GETS BETTER IS A STORY.

THE NEGATIVE HALF, WHICH NOBODY STATES.  The same source, compiled
for THREE TARGETS at the SAME optimisation level.  How much of the IR
is COMMON and how much is TARGET-SPECIFIC:

  MEASURED, and the level that shows it is -Os, so the table is over
  all five levels and not only the one that shows it.

  level   target          IR insns   ops   shared with the other two
  O0      x86_64               260    20   20 of 20
  O0      aarch64              260    20   20 of 20
  O0      riscv64              260    20   20 of 20
  O1      x86_64                99    17   17 of 17
  O1      aarch64               99    17   17 of 17
  O1      riscv64               99    17   17 of 17
  O2      x86_64               207    19   19 of 19
  O2      aarch64              207    19   19 of 19
  O2      riscv64              203    19   19 of 19
  O3      x86_64               207    19   19 of 19
  O3      aarch64              207    19   19 of 19
  O3      riscv64              203    19   19 of 19
  Os      x86_64                99    17   17 of 17
  Os      aarch64               99    17   17 of 17
  Os      riscv64              151    19   17 of 19

  AT -Os THE THREE TARGETS SHARE 17 OF 19 OPCODES, AND THE 2 THAT
  ARE TARGET-SPECIFIC ARE:
    insertelement      riscv64
    shufflevector      riscv64

  §KEEP§AND THE COUNTS ARE PRINTED BESIDE EVERY PERCENTAGE, BECAUSE A
  PERCENTAGE IN A SENTENCE IS A NUMBER NOBODY CAN CHECK.

  §KEEP§AND THIS IS NOT A QUIRK OF THE CORPUS. §KEEP§THE SAME SOURCE
  AT THE SAME LEVEL VECTORISES ON ONE TARGET AND NOT ON ANOTHER, AND
  THE ONLY EVIDENCE OF THAT IS TWO OPCODES THAT APPEAR IN ONE IR AND
  NOT IN THE OTHERS. §KEEP§IT IS ALSO WHY THE FINDING BELOW IS NOT
  "AN IR IS TARGET-INDEPENDENT" AND NOT "AN IR IS TARGET-DEPENDENT":
  AT -O2 ALL THREE AGREE ON EVERY OPCODE, AND THE MACHINE CODES STILL
  DIFFER.

  THE MACHINE CODES AT -O2, WHICH DISAGREE DESPITE THE IR AGREEING:

  target          IR insns   machine insns   IR/machine
  x86_64               207             253        0.818
  aarch64              207             190        1.089
  riscv64              203             207        0.981

  §KEEP§THREE TARGETS, ONE OPCODE SET, THREE DIFFERENT INSTRUCTION
  COUNTS. §KEEP§AND THE RATIO IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT: THE IR IS THE
  SAME AND THE CODE IS NOT, AND EVERY INSTRUCTION OF THE DIFFERENCE IS
  A DECISION THE BACKEND MADE.

WHAT AN IR THEREFORE DOES AND DOES NOT BUY, and the second half is the
one this course exists for:

  DOES: one representation that every backend reads, so an
        optimisation is written once and benefits from targets that
        did not exist when it was written. §KEEP§AND THAT IS MEASURED
        HERE: 17 of 19 opcodes are common to all three targets.
  DOES NOT: hide the target.  The IR is target-INDEPENDENT in its
        OPERATIONS and target-DEPENDENT in its SHAPES, and the shapes
        are where a backend lives: vector WIDTH, the width of an
        integer, and whether a multiply is fused.

WHAT REPLACES LLVM, BY FILE NAME, because the mission says a learner
should be able to build this without any backend at all:

  LLVM's job                        the file here that does it
  ---------------------------------  --------------------------------
  textual IR (parse and print)      cbir.py  (read_llvm_ir, above)
  the IR itself                      cbir.py  (Ins, is_reg, simulate)
  instruction selection              cbir.py  (isel_tree, PATTERNS)
  register allocation, linear scan   cbreg.py  (alloc_linear_scan)
  register allocation, colouring     cbreg.py  (alloc_colour)
  liveness and interference          cbreg.py  (build_interference)
  spill slots and reloads            cbreg.py  (pack_spill_slots)
  coalescing                         cbreg.py  (coalesce)
  instruction scheduling             cbsched.py (build_dag, list_schedule)
  the ENCODER                        cbenc.py  (enc_a64_madd, enc_x86)
  the machine we measure on          cbbench.c  (the timing instrument)

AND WHAT IS STILL MISSING, printed rather than implied:
  * SSA construction and PHI placement.  Our IR is straight-line and
    the reason is in cbir.py's own docstring: instruction selection,
    allocation and scheduling are three questions about a SEQUENCE of
    operations, and a branch would put a fourth question in the
    middle of them.
  * A real CFG.  No basic blocks, no dominators, no loop structure.
  * Peepholes, and the x86-64 and RISC-V compressed encodings.
  * Exception frames and debug information.


==========================================================================
SECTION 3 -- THE SAME FUNCTION AT -O0 AND AT -O2, INSTRUCTION BY
INSTRUCTION, AND WHERE THE VECTORISER ENTERED.
==========================================================================

WHICH FUNCTION.  `walk`, because it is the only one in the corpus with
a reduction the vectoriser can take AND a non-reduction the vectoriser
cannot, so the level at which it fires is visible as a CHANGE rather
than as an absence.

  -O0    27 instructions, 112 bytes, 0 SSE/AVX operands
  -O1    18 instructions,  64 bytes, 0 SSE/AVX operands
  -O2    59 instructions, 222 bytes, 49 SSE/AVX operands
  -O3    59 instructions, 222 bytes, 49 SSE/AVX operands
  -Os    16 instructions,  42 bytes, 0 SSE/AVX operands

THE VECTORISER, WHICH IS NOT A FLAG AND IS NOT AT -O1.  Measured over
the WHOLE corpus, once per level, for each target:

  level   target          IR vector ops   machine vector insns
  O0      x86_64                       0                      0
  O0      aarch64                      0                      0
  O0      riscv64                      0                      0
  O1      x86_64                       0                      0
  O1      aarch64                      0                      0
  O1      riscv64                      0                      0
  O2      x86_64                       2                     42
  O2      aarch64                      2                     14
  O2      riscv64                      2                     20
  O3      x86_64                       2                     42
  O3      aarch64                      2                     14
  O3      riscv64                      2                     20
  Os      x86_64                       0                      0
  Os      aarch64                      0                      0
  Os      riscv64                      2                     26

  IR vector operations across all three targets:  -O1 0, -O2 6, -O3 6, -Os 2.

  §KEEP§AND THE FINDING IS THE SAME ONE THE RISC-V ATOMICS COURSE
  MEASURED, IN A DIFFERENT DIRECTION AND IT IS THE SECOND TIME THIS
  COLLECTION HAS SEEN IT: §KEEP§-Os VECTORISES ON ONE TARGET AND NOT
  ON THE OTHER TWO, AND -Os EMITS MORE IR FOR RISC-V THAN -O2 DOES.
  §KEEP§A LEVEL OF -O IS NOT A RANKING. §KEEP§IT IS A SET OF
  OBJECTIVES, AND THE VECTORISER IS THE MOST CAPABILITY-SENSITIVE PASS
  IN THE COMPILER.

  THE ONE FUNCTION, -O0 AGAINST -O2, THE WHOLE OF IT:

  ---- -O0, 27 instructions ----
     240  55                     pushq	%rbp
