  THE DIFFERENCE IN ONE LINE: -O0 emits 27 instructions and -O2 emits
  59 -- MORE, NOT FEWER -- and §KEEP§THE INSTRUCTIONS THAT APPEARED
  ARE NOT RANDOM EITHER.
  §KEEP§THEY ARE THE ONES THAT EXISTED ONLY TO GIVE THE STACK A
  SHAPE: -O0 SPILLS EVERY ARGUMENT AND -O2 SPILLS NOTHING IN A LEAF.

WHAT THIS SECTION CANNOT SHOW, and the limit is the same one the whole
course is built around:
  * it cannot show that either schedule is FAST.  It is a COUNT.
    §KEEP§THE TIMING IS IN SECTION 10 AND IT IS THE ONLY TIMING IN
    THE COURSE THAT IS ABOUT SOMETHING OTHER THAN A REGISTER.
  * it cannot show which of the three targets a backend is better at.
    §KEEP§IT CAN SHOW THAT THEY DIFFER, WHICH IS A WEAKER CLAIM AND A
    TRUE ONE.


==========================================================================
SECTION 4 -- INSTRUCTION SELECTION: THE TREE WALK, AND THE FAILURE
MODE NOBODY NAMES.
==========================================================================

THE SELECTOR IS A TREE WALK over operand KINDS, and it is written in
cbir.py: a pattern is (opcode, kind), a machine form is selected by
dispatching on the pair, and the destination may be reused as an input
because every arithmetic form on x86-64 is two-address.

  THE VOCABULARY IS TWELVE OPERATIONS and it is the same twelve on all
  three targets, which is the entire point of having an IR.  §KEEP§A
  real backend has a hundred more, and the limit is stated in
  cbir.py's docstring rather than left for a reader to discover.

THE SELECTOR, RUN, over all three kernels:

  kernel   instructions   isel_tree   isel_kind   MISROUTED
  kernel_a            12          12          12          10
  kernel_b            17          17          17          12
  kernel_c            16          16          16          13
  kernel_d            16          16          16          14
  TOTAL               61          61          61          49

  §KEEP§THE MISROUTE COLUMN IS THE CLASSIC TRAP AND IT IS NOT A
  TYPO. §KEEP§A SELECTOR THAT DISPATCHES ON OPERAND KIND ALONE TAKES
  THE FIRST PATTERN WITH A MATCHING KIND, WHICH IN THE TABLE IN
  cbir.py IS `add` FOR EVERY TWO-OPERAND ARITHMETIC FORM -- §KEEP§SO
  EVERY `sub`, `xor`, `shl` AND `mul` IN THE CORPUS IS ROUTED TO THE
  INSTRUCTION FOR `add`. §KEEP§THE RESULT IS NOT A CRASH: IT IS A
  PROGRAM THAT COMPUTES A DIFFERENT NUMBER, AND THE SELECTOR NEVER
  FAILS ONCE. §KEEP§THAT IS WHY THE CHECKSUM BELOW IS THE RECEIPT AND
  NOT THE COUNT.

  THE FIRST MISROUTED INSTRUCTION, printed so the mechanism is not a
  paragraph:
    instruction 0 is      v0 = mov 7
    its operand kind is    ri
    isel_tree  emits       mov       <- correct
    isel_kind  emits       add       <- the bug

    §KEEP§BOTH ROUTES HAVE THE SAME OPERAND KIND, WHICH IS WHY
    THE KIND TREE MATCHES ONE WITH THE PATTERN FOR THE OTHER:
    §KEEP§`mov` AND `add` HAVE THE SAME OPERANDS, §KEEP§AND A
    DISPATCH THAT CANNOT SEE THE OPCODE CANNOT SEE THE
    DIFFERENCE. §KEEP§AND NEITHER OF THEM EVER FAILS.

THE CHECKSUM, WHICH IS THE RECEIPT AND NOT THE COUNT:

  the correct program folds to  193715795505516148
  one sub computed as an add    193715795509322676
  §KEEP§THE TWO NUMBERS DIFFER, AND THAT IS THE ONLY SENTENCE IN THIS
  SECTION THAT MATTERS: §KEEP§A MISROUTED INSTRUCTION IS NOT A BAD
  INSTRUCTION SELECTION, IT IS A DIFFERENT PROGRAM.


==========================================================================
SECTION 5 -- FUSION FROM THE IR, AND THE RECEIPT IS BYTES.
==========================================================================

THE CLAIM.  A multiply-accumulate is ONE instruction on AArch64 and TWO
on x86-64, and the difference is not a scheduling decision: it is the
OPERAND COUNT in the encoding.

MEASURED-ON-BYTES, and built BY HAND rather than assembled, because an
assembler hides the question.  cbenc.py encodes four AArch64
instructions from register numbers with a mask table and NO LIBRARY,
and every field position in that table is checked against six words a
real assembler emitted.

  THE ENCODER AGAINST A REAL ASSEMBLER, six specimens, bit for bit:

    instruction                      assembler    this file   agree
    madd  x0, x1, x2, x3            9b020c20   9b020c20   yes
    msub  x4, x5, x6, x7            9b069ca4   9b069ca4   yes
    add   x8, x9, x10, lsl #1       8b0a0528   8b0a0528   yes
    add   x11, x12, x13, lsl #2     8b0d098b   8b0d098b   yes
    madd  w0, w1, w2, w3            1b020c20   1b020c20   yes
    add   x0, x1, #0xfff            913ffc20   913ffc20   yes
    movz  w10, #0x6                 528000ca   528000ca   yes

    7 of 7 agree.  §KEEP§THIS IS NOT A CROSS-CHECK IN THE TWO-READER
    SENSE -- IT IS AN ENCODER AGAINST AN ASSEMBLER -- §KEEP§SO IT IS A
    DIFFERENT KIND OF EVIDENCE AND IT IS LABELLED AS ONE. §KEEP§IT IS
    ALSO WHAT CAUGHT ALL THREE BUGS IN THE TABLE ABOVE, NONE OF WHICH
    RAISED AN ERROR.

  THE FUSED FORM, ENCODED HERE:

    madd x0, x1, x0, x2   = 0x9b000820   4 bytes
    add  x0, x1, x2       = 0x8b020020   4 bytes
    ret                   = 0xd65f03c0   4 bytes

  THE OPERAND COUNT IS IN THE WORD, and here it is being read OUT of
  the word by two programs that were written for different jobs:

    word         this file's fields   this file's text        the sibling decoder says
    0x9b000820   0,1,0,2            madd x0, x1, x0, x2      madd x0, x1, x0, x2
    0x8b020020   0,1,2,0            add x0, x1, x2           add x0, x1, x2
    0xd65f03c0   0,30,31,0          ret                      ret

    3 of 3 agree.  §KEEP§THE SIBLING DECODER IS rvasm/a64asm'S
    OWN FILE, WRITTEN FOR A DIFFERENT COURSE, AND IT HAS NEVER
    HEARD OF cbenc.py. §KEEP§THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE AGREEMENT
    EVIDENCE INSTEAD OF A RESTATEMENT.

  §KEEP§AND NOW THE ACTUAL FINDING, WHICH IS NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE.
  FOUR FIVE-BIT REGISTER FIELDS ARE TWENTY BITS OF THIRTY-TWO. §KEEP§THE
  REMAINING TWELVE HOLD sf, A TEN-BIT GROUP SELECTOR, THE MADD/MSUB
  BIT AND THE SHIFT AMOUNT. §KEEP§THE THREE-OPERAND FORM IS NOT A
  COMPRESSED FORM AND NOT A COMPROMISE; IT IS THE SAME WIDTH WITH ONE
  FIELD READING 31, WHICH IS HOW THE ENCODING SAYS "NO FOURTH
  OPERAND".

  THE x86-64 SIDE, FROM cbenc.py, AND IT IS A REFUSAL:

    imul rbx, rcx  ->  48 0f af cb
    add  rbx, rdx  ->  48 01 d3

    enc_x86('imul', 'rax', 'rbx', 'rcx') is the call a
    backend would have to make -- a THREE-OPERAND multiply -- and:
      enc_x86 takes (op, dst, src) and NOTHING ELSE; x86-64 has no three-opera

      §KEEP§AND THE FAILURE MODE WORTH NAMING: §KEEP§THE SIGNATURE IS
      `enc_x86(op, dst, src, rex_w=True)`, §KEEP§SO A CALL WITH FOUR
      POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS BINDS THE FOURTH TO `rex_w` -- WHICH IS
      A TRUTHY STRING -- §KEEP§AND WITHOUT AN EXPLICIT ARITY CHECK
      IT ENCODED A TWO-OPERAND INSTRUCTION WHEN THE CALLER ASKED
      FOR THREE. §KEEP§A CALL THAT LOOKS LIKE IT IS BEING REJECTED
      AND IS INSTEAD SILENTLY ACCEPTED IS THE WORST SHAPE A
      FUNCTION SIGNATURE CAN HAVE, §KEEP§AND IT PRODUCED A
      PLAUSIBLE ENCODING RATHER THAN AN ERROR.
      §KEEP§THE FUNCTION TAKES TWO OPERANDS AND CANNOT EXPRESS A
      THREE-OPERAND MULTIPLY, AND THAT IS NOT A GAP IN cbenc.py --
      IT IS A PROPERTY OF x86-64. §KEEP§THE MODRM BYTE IS WHERE IT
      LIVES: BITS 3 TO 5 ARE THE SOURCE AND THE LOW THREE ARE THE
      DESTINATION, SO THE ENCODER PUTS THE DESTINATION WHERE THE
      BASE FIELD IS AND THE SOURCE WHERE THE SHIFTED FIELD IS.

  §KEEP§SO THE ACCUMULATOR HAS TO SURVIVE THE FIRST INSTRUCTION AS A
  REGISTER NAME, AND THE ONLY WAY x86-64 FUSES `a*b+c` IS TO PUT THE
  ADD IN THE MODRM OF THE IMUL -- WHICH IS A DIFFERENT INSTRUCTION
  ENCODING ENTIRELY. §KEEP§THE TWO INSTRUCTIONS ARE NOT ONE INSTRUCTION
  WITH A DIFFERENT MNEMONIC; THEY ARE A DIFFERENT OPCODE.

  AND WHAT clang ACTUALLY EMITTED, for the same C, as a third reader:

    aarch64   0 instructions, 0 bytes
    x86_64    9 instructions, 31 bytes
         0  48 8d 04 77    leaq	(%rdi,%rsi,2), %rax
         4  48 8d 14 52    leaq	(%rdx,%rdx,2), %rdx
         8  48 01 c2       addq	%rax, %rdx
         b  48 8d 04 8a    leaq	(%rdx,%rcx,4), %rax
         f  4b 8d 0c 80    leaq	(%r8,%r8,4), %rcx
        13  48 01 c1       addq	%rax, %rcx
        16  4b 8d 04 49    leaq	(%r9,%r9,2), %rax
        1a  48 8d 04 41    leaq	(%rcx,%rax,2), %rax
        1e  c3             retq

  §KEEP§AND THE AArch64 WORD clang EMITTED IS THE SAME WORD cbenc.py
  ENCODED, WHICH IS THE POINT OF HAVING AN ENCODER: §KEEP§THE SAME
  0x9b000820, FROM FOUR REGISTER NUMBERS, WITH NO TABLE AND NO LIBRARY.

WHAT THIS SECTION CANNOT SHOW: it cannot show that FUSING IS FASTER.
§KEEP§TWO INSTRUCTIONS THAT DEPEND ON EACH OTHER COST ONE MORE CYCLE
THAN ONE THAT DOES NOT, AND THE TIMING FOR THAT IS IN SECTION 9 AND
THE NUMBER THERE IS NOT ATTRIBUTED TO FUSING ALONE.


==========================================================================
SECTION 6 -- REGISTER ALLOCATION: THREE ALLOCATORS, THREE SPILL
POLICIES, AND TWO DELIBERATE BUGS.  This is the centrepiece.
==========================================================================

THREE ALLOCATORS, ALL WRITTEN HERE, ALL ON THE SAME IR:

  linear scan     Braun & Hack, CACM 21(10) 1978.  Sorts intervals by
                  start point and needs NO interference graph -- only
                  the intervals, which can be built in one pass.
  Chaitin colour  Chaitin/Briggs/Panter/Scholz 1979.  Simplify, select,
                  spill, on a graph whose nodes are virtual registers
                  and whose edges are interference.
  colour, iterate  Greedy colour first, spill what will not colour,
                  repeat.  §KEEP§THE THIRD IS NOT IN THE TEXTBOOK AND
                  IT EXISTS HERE BECAUSE THE THREE SPILL COUNTS
                  DISAGREE, AND A COMPARISON THAT QUOTES ONE NUMBER
                  WITHOUT SAYING WHICH POLICY PRODUCED IT HAS NOT
                  QUOTED A REPRODUCIBLE NUMBER AT ALL.

